Section: 5. Legal Sector > Workload Model

All the studies constructed their analysis of costs in dif- ferent ways and this reflects differences in availability of administrative data or country-specific research. However, despite this multiplicity there were, loosely, four methodo- logical configurations for estimating criminal justice costs:

1. Workload model;

2. Event model;

3. Purpose-built economic model;

4. Eclectic model.

A workload model basis its estimation directly on time-based units of work, such as the cost of a service per worked hour or per worked day. In a workload model the focus is on the average unit of time spent, such as an hour or a day, on a specific event task, and the cost of this unit of time. The monetary value per time-task period is then multiplied by the number of event tasks. Three studies employed a workload model and an overview of their methodologies is summarised in Table 5.2.2.1.

Table 5.2.2.1: Workload model methodologies: criminal justice system

